# Python

## Design

- Use type hints on public functions and dataclass fields.
- Model site data with frozen dataclasses. Use `Decimal` for meter values and timezone-aware datetimes for timestamps.
- Raise a specific exception from `odecet_info.client.errors`. Do not use bare `except`.
- Keep I/O in the client. Parsers accept text and return readings. They do not open sockets.
- If a new column, unit, or meter type appears, extend the existing mapping tables. Do not add a second parser for the same format.
- A bad row is skipped and recorded as a warning. A bad login or an unrecognizable document fails the whole fetch.
- Comments explain why a guard exists (honeypot, antiforgery, locale). Do not restate the code.

## Style

- Format with Ruff. Line length is 100.
- Prefer `pathlib`, `datetime.timezone`, and stdlib `csv` / `html.parser`.
- No blocking calls inside async functions. HTTP uses `aiohttp`.
- Tests are deterministic. No test contacts odecet.info. Fixtures are synthetic.

## Errors worth naming

Separate authentication failure, local validation, rate limiting, transport failure, and a document the parser does not recognize. Callers in Home Assistant map those differently (reauth, repair, retry).
